findings.body:
The new `automation_triggered_event_id` validator on `TriggeredAction` rejects any non-None id when `triggering_event` is None. `triggers.act()` always passes `primary_state_change_event_id` (non-None for Triggered), so for proactive firings with no triggering event, `TriggeredAction(...)` raises `ValidationError` before anything is published. That aborts `act()` and stops every action on that automation.

I reproduced this by building a `TriggeredAction` with `triggering_event=None` and a UUID for `automation_triggered_event_id`. The validator raised. The existing `test_acting_publishes_an_action_message_from_a_proactive_trigger` calls `triggers.act()` with `triggering_event=None`, so it hits the same path and should now fail.

::callout{type="warning"}
The validator is the wrong guard. Proactive firings legitimately have no triggering event, and the link to the automation.triggered event is still valid there. Drop the validator, or relax it so that a None triggering_event is allowed.

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/schemas/automations.py" lines="772-780"}

The validator is also redundant with the `if triggered_action.automation_triggered_event_id:` guards in `actions.py`, which already handle a missing triggering event.

`succeed()` previously built an inline `Resource` with three keys. The PR replaces it with the `resource` variable, which `Action.succeed()` and `fail()` share. That variable adds `prefect.posture` for `EventTrigger` automations.

The result is that `action.triggered` and `action.executed` events for event-triggered automations gain a `prefect.posture` attribute. The PR description does not mention this, and it is not needed for the linking feature. Existing consumers that match on the resource shape could see a change. The refactor is reasonable, but the behavior change should either be called out or dropped.

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/actions.py" lines="286-304"}
